nsortium is now 11 years old  they have identified that it is  really a barrier to the implementation  of pharmacogenetics  of how to interpret what is a physician  to  do with a genotype when it is available  and to really address this barrier  the cpic was formed to  provide guidelines that enable the  translation of genetic laboratory tests  into actionable prescribing decisions  and so it is important to understand  that the cpic does not recommend weather  testing  should be done but it is designed to  help clinicians understand  how an available genetic test result can  be used to optimize  a drug therapy  so what you see here is a table one  um from sap guideline  and um all these tables one are  very similar in all these guidelines and  so you see uh the phenotype definitions  like  ultrarapid normal or intermediate  metabolizer  for cytochrome p450 2d6 that is the  activity score system that is used to  facilitate the translation of genotype  into a phenotype  there is a brief description of the  genome type  and then to your right you see examples  of  c26 diplotypes and again you see here  the star nomenclature  and these go back to these definitions  that are listed in foam bar so  we are working very closely with the  cpeg  again to standardize the um  usage of these star alleles very  consistently  among the sea peak farm gkb and bonbar  and this is also a reason why we have a  fairly high standards before we define  an allelic variant  to make sure that it is the best  possible definition  because we know that c peak might  eventually  pick up an allelic variant and is basing  our  clinical recommendations on an allele  then clinical test reports use star  nomenclature  so these two examples are very  generic examples reports you might see  and tests might report  star alleles in a table form or as you  can see here  on the right side also list the genotype  and then give you a little range of  where the phenotype might be  and so again it is very important that  unified nomenclature is used for these  test reports  because otherwise it is really  impossible  to interpret these especially across  providers as  patients might leave one institution or  switch  physicians so there is definitely still  a lot of work to do  to make that more unified and  standardized  then of course there are so many  different clinical labs out there  and everybody is doing testing slightly  different  by method or what they test for  and so the association of molecular  pathology has started  to come up with clinical genotype allele  selection  recommendations they are using a  two-tiered system  so tier one is a minimum panel of  alleles that should be  tested tier two is an extended panel of  um  variants and their selections for these  tiers is really based on how frequent  these are  whether an allele occurs in just one or  more populations  whether they are testing materials  available  and so on and so forth and they also use  of course our font var allele  definitions if they are available  and so for  cytogram p450 2c19  they have already published  recommendations and they also have  published recommendations for  2c9 and recommendations are underway  for cytogram p450 and 2d6  moving on to the get rm project  so this is housed at the cdc  and is tasked to develop reference  materials for pcx testing  this is needless to say very important  for clinical laboratories  but of course having good reference  materials is also  important for anything going on in  our research so they collaborate with  the coriol  repositories because these dnas  are widely available and  these groups these groups enlist  commercial and academic laboratories to  perform a pcx uh  testing each sample is tested by  multiple platforms by different  groups to come then up with a consensus  um genotype and they had two um  landmark papers before and then a third  paper was  uh published last fall on soup 2d6  where um 42 new samples  have been added to the list many of  those  containing rare alleles and unusual  genotypes  and this is what you can see here in  table four  and the highlight also is that these  consensus genotypes  are reported out as a star nomenclature  so again emphasizing that you do need  a resource that keeps track of all these  star alleles and sub-alleles and so on  and so forth  because um all these different projects  and groups  rely on it  then there is also tool and algorithm  developments  to interpret genotype data so there are  a number of commercial platforms which  also provide calling tools for these  star alleles  and then there are also independent  academically  developed tools to interpret for example  next generation  sequencing data like australia stargazer  only  uh to name just um these three  and to your left you see uh roughly how  that works  so um in that red box is the allele  definition so these are tables  that go into these algorithms and then  you have your um  genotype data in different formats and  then the algorithm is doing its thing  and is reporting out these diplotypes  based on the allele definitions  that it received as an input and so you  don't need to be a bioinformatician  to understand that the better and  more high quality data the little  definitions are  the better are these interpreted um  diplo types and algorithms  itself so again uh  the more allele variants we are able to  define and the more granularly we can  define them  the better will these tools uh work to  call these  data and uh provides reliable  um diplo type calls  and then here on the last slide i want  to briefly introduce  the farm cat initiative so the farm  gkb together with cpic realized that it  would really  be terrific to have one pipeline  that uses a genotype data which is fed  uh into the algorithm then use the  farmvar allele definitions see  pig clinical recommendations so that a  final report  takes everything together so that you  have one  pathway in one pipeline from the  genotype or sequence data  to a final report for a patient  and so i hope i gave you um  some insights of how complex it is to  really  make precision medicine work so  we are all really in this together and  although i put  farm bar here in the middle piece there  are so many puzzle pieces  that have to come together whether it's  basic research  medical research um education  the pharmacy kb pharmcat ptx testing  physicians to make  this happen for the individualized  for the patient to receive an  individualized a drug prescribing  and move this precision medicine  forward and with this i would like to  thank you for your attention  and i'm happy to take any questions
 

